       When doing  operations  on  a  filesystem  configuration  context,  the
       filesystem  driver may choose to provide ancillary information to user‐
       space in the form of message strings.

       The filesystem  context  file  descriptors  returned  by  ffssooppeenn()  and
       ffssppiicckk(2)  may  be  queried  for message strings at any time by calling
       rreeaadd(2) on the file descriptor.  Each call to  rreeaadd(2)  will  return  a
       single message, prefixed to indicate its class:

              ee _m_e_s_s_a_g_e
                     An  error message was logged.  This is usually associated
                     with an error being returned from the corresponding  sys‐
                     tem call which triggered this message.

              ww _m_e_s_s_a_g_e
                     A warning message was logged.

              ii _m_e_s_s_a_g_e
                     An informational message was logged.

       Messages  are  removed  from the queue as they are read.  Note that the
       message queue has limited depth, so it is possible for messages to  get
       lost.   If there are no messages in the message queue, rreeaadd((22)) will re‐
       turn -1 and _e_r_r_n_o will be set to  EENNOODDAATTAA.   If  the  _b_u_f  argument  to
       rreeaadd(2) is not large enough to contain the entire message, rreeaadd(2) will
       return -1 and _e_r_r_n_o will be set to EEMMSSGGSSIIZZEE.  (See BUGS.)

       If there are multiple filesystem contexts referencing the same filesys‐
       tem instance (such as if you call ffssppiicckk(2) multiple times for the same
       mount), each one gets its own independent message queue.  This does not
       apply to multiple file descriptors that are tied to the same underlying
       open file description (such as those created with dduupp(2)).

       Message  strings will usually be prefixed by the name of the filesystem
       or kernel subsystem that logged the message, though this may not always
       be the case.  See the Linux kernel source code for details.

